1
00:01:27,170 --> 00:01:31,070
 The Carolingian Empire crumbled. Europe was attacked from all sides.

2
00:01:31,650 --> 00:01:36,690
 In the south, the Saracens invaded Crete, Sicily, Tuscany, Rome, and Provence,

3
00:01:36,830 --> 00:01:39,030
 where a beachhead was set up near Saint-Tropez.

4
00:01:39,950 --> 00:01:46,250
 From the Asiatic steppes, the Magyars crossed the Danube Plain and headed for Lombardy,

5
00:01:46,370 --> 00:01:48,970
 Venetia, Bavaria, Burgundy, Provence, and Rome.

6
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:52,810
 And from the north came waves of Vikings, or Normans,

7
00:01:52,810 --> 00:01:55,550
 who appeared on European soil in the early 800s.

8
00:01:56,040 --> 00:01:59,690
 But let's not go too fast. Let's go back to 790.

9
00:02:00,540 --> 00:02:05,320
 We are about to pay a visit to these fierce warriors, these lords of the sea.

10
00:02:27,710 --> 00:02:39,040
 Life seems more peaceful than warlike.

11
00:02:50,930 --> 00:02:53,760
 Our friend Lurch is a trail, a born serf.

12
00:02:54,450 --> 00:02:56,930
 Bert is a bond, a free man who carries a sword.

13
00:02:57,430 --> 00:02:59,920
 Here he comes to give his trusted servant a hand.

14
00:03:16,980 --> 00:03:20,390
 We manage.

15
00:03:21,030 --> 00:03:21,660
 Try this.

16
00:03:21,950 --> 00:03:23,610
 Oh, good idea.

17
00:03:26,210 --> 00:03:29,510
 Here comes Jarl, or Chief.

18
00:03:29,530 --> 00:03:31,130
 All right, all right.

19
00:03:32,450 --> 00:03:35,490
 Accompanied by his own trusted manservant.

20
00:03:36,840 --> 00:03:37,200
 Do.

21
00:03:43,210 --> 00:03:52,990
 Don't make fun of the master.

22
00:03:52,990 --> 00:03:54,070
 Yeah, this'll do some good.

23
00:03:54,550 --> 00:03:56,230
 Slave, come here.

24
00:03:56,430 --> 00:03:56,820
 What?

25
00:03:56,920 --> 00:03:58,050
 What's so funny?

26
00:03:58,050 --> 00:03:59,930
 That's my own man-servant.

27
00:03:59,930 --> 00:04:04,560
 Hmm.

28
00:04:04,560 --> 00:04:13,100
 That's knotted scarf shows that she's a married woman.

29
00:04:13,100 --> 00:04:18,810
 The chain of keys and scissors she wears shows that she's also the degia, or mistress of the house.

30
00:04:18,810 --> 00:04:44,590
 Come in.

31
00:04:44,590 --> 00:04:52,030
 Wait.

32
00:04:52,030 --> 00:04:57,510
 I've got a brilliant solution.

33
00:04:57,510 --> 00:04:58,620
 There you go.

34
00:04:58,620 --> 00:05:03,970
 Minute.

35
00:05:03,970 --> 00:05:09,340
 No, period. Monogamy hasn't been invented yet. You'll just have to wait.

36
00:05:09,340 --> 00:05:12,640
 Roasted seaweed.

37
00:05:12,640 --> 00:05:15,850
 That's all I've got left.

38
00:05:15,850 --> 00:05:19,360
 Before long, we'll have all the whales we want. Just big.

39
00:05:19,360 --> 00:05:25,760
 Hey, why don't we have a beer?

40
00:05:25,760 --> 00:05:30,010
 Here you go, man.

41
00:05:30,010 --> 00:05:34,050
 Oh, no, not you. You'll have to wait, 'til you grow up.

42
00:05:34,050 --> 00:05:40,420
 Whoa!

43
00:05:40,420 --> 00:05:50,530
 These newborns have spent the night lying unprotected outside the village stockade.

44
00:05:50,530 --> 00:06:05,290
 Weighs a ton!

45
00:06:05,290 --> 00:06:30,080
 ...orders all the men leave to begin the fishing season.

46
00:06:30,080 --> 00:06:35,970
 Keith's wives and his children, legitimate and otherwise, have come to wave goodbye to their lord and master.

47
00:06:35,970 --> 00:06:42,730
 Prepares birchbark bread, food for times of famine.

48
00:06:42,730 --> 00:06:46,870
 What do you say we go hunting for Iderdale?

49
00:06:46,870 --> 00:06:49,820
 I'm ready when you are.

50
00:06:49,820 --> 00:06:53,690
 Let's go!

51
00:06:53,690 --> 00:06:56,430
 Wait up!

52
00:06:56,430 --> 00:07:02,650
 Doesn't pay, my boy.

53
00:07:02,650 --> 00:07:07,490
 Hold on a minute, children. Here. These are for you.

54
00:07:18,050 --> 00:07:21,630
 Try and keep still.

55
00:07:49,950 --> 00:07:50,590
 See whales.

56
00:07:50,750 --> 00:07:50,970
 What?

57
00:07:51,540 --> 00:07:52,980
 I'm telling you.

58
00:07:53,250 --> 00:07:53,530
 Huh?

59
00:07:54,590 --> 00:07:59,730
 You say so.

60
00:08:00,850 --> 00:08:02,220
 A single one up from a group.

61
00:08:58,050 --> 00:09:36,670
 Full basket of down. Give it to me.

62
00:09:44,040 --> 00:09:52,400
 You could do just as well.

63
00:09:55,690 --> 00:10:02,240
 By the time the boats head for home, all that is left on the shore is a pile of bones.

64
00:10:04,920 --> 00:10:07,390
 And so the hunt has been a successful one.

65
00:10:07,880 --> 00:10:09,220
 The race is on to reach home.

66
00:10:11,070 --> 00:10:14,650
 We'll pay for that.

67
00:10:15,540 --> 00:10:17,130
 Just you wait, you.

68
00:10:17,130 --> 00:10:28,440
 The festival of the summer solstice will begin.

69
00:10:29,400 --> 00:10:31,440
 Maestro, the wise elder, gives the signal.

70
00:10:31,910 --> 00:10:39,190
 In front of the altar to the gods, Thor, by his side, Odin, and Frey.

71
00:10:40,830 --> 00:10:41,370
 Kneel!

72
00:10:44,720 --> 00:10:46,470
 Peace, you're right there!

73
00:10:47,530 --> 00:10:49,880
 This meat or strong deer here.

74
00:10:51,560 --> 00:10:51,920
 Taste.

75
00:10:54,210 --> 00:10:55,350
 Have to make sure it's good.

76
00:10:56,290 --> 00:10:56,930
 This.

77
00:10:59,800 --> 00:11:01,660
 You're good, very strong.

78
00:11:05,850 --> 00:11:06,840
 Some more.

79
00:11:07,150 --> 00:11:08,210
 Just a little sip.

80
00:11:08,700 --> 00:11:09,600
 See what happens.

81
00:11:09,780 --> 00:11:09,890
 Yeah.

82
00:11:14,810 --> 00:11:19,750
 Anything about booze.

83
00:11:19,890 --> 00:11:21,750
 I never drank that before, never.

84
00:11:21,750 --> 00:11:24,680
 In the foreground, we see the men's table.

85
00:11:24,980 --> 00:11:27,420
 The women sit in the back with the children in the middle.

86
00:11:28,210 --> 00:11:30,230
 And here are the menfolk dressed up for the occasion.

87
00:11:43,970 --> 00:11:46,330
 Toast to the great feast of the summer solstice.

88
00:11:47,070 --> 00:11:49,780
 And the gods who hold me in their favor.

89
00:12:01,450 --> 00:12:17,730
 I was a little bird and a little ranch who did it, not me.

90
00:12:18,190 --> 00:12:23,060
 Right, my fine fellows, you not only insult my son, you dare to insult me, your chief.

91
00:12:23,400 --> 00:12:25,280
 Won't be long, you'll see who's boss here.

92
00:12:25,720 --> 00:12:27,480
 Doom make examples for all the others.

93
00:12:27,580 --> 00:12:28,200
 Hold on there.

94
00:12:28,410 --> 00:12:29,080
 Come over here.

95
00:12:29,080 --> 00:12:29,900
 One moment.

96
00:12:30,040 --> 00:12:31,200
 Just a minute, my friends.

97
00:12:31,320 --> 00:12:35,190
 According to the law, this must be settled by a duel.

98
00:12:35,190 --> 00:12:41,010
 It was the custom for the opponents to meet at the Yggdrasil, or a Tree of Life, where the duel would take place.

99
00:12:41,010 --> 00:12:45,350
 The distance will be 15 meters.

100
00:12:45,350 --> 00:12:46,510
 Good.

101
00:12:46,510 --> 00:12:48,700
 Here, catch this.

102
00:12:48,700 --> 00:12:49,520
 What the?

103
00:12:49,520 --> 00:12:50,920
 Good, at my signal.

104
00:12:50,920 --> 00:12:52,110
 Ready or not?

105
00:12:52,110 --> 00:12:54,400
 One, two, uh, one second!

106
00:12:54,400 --> 00:13:03,780
 Have to go on to the next test.

107
00:13:03,780 --> 00:13:08,590
 Here, go ahead.

108
00:13:10,350 --> 00:13:24,190
 Turn.

109
00:13:24,190 --> 00:13:28,560
 Why don't you?

110
00:13:28,560 --> 00:13:31,350
 What are you waiting for?

111
00:13:32,250 --> 00:13:34,330
 Oh no, it's unbelievable.

112
00:13:34,330 --> 00:13:35,030
 I won!

113
00:13:35,030 --> 00:13:42,610
 Uh...

114
00:13:42,610 --> 00:13:49,240
 Chief has disgraced the Viking's honor. Banish him!

115
00:13:49,240 --> 00:13:52,920
 Yeah! Chief has no respect for the Viking's laws! He's got to leave!

116
00:13:52,920 --> 00:13:58,220
 Understood. They put you out. You're now an outlaw. You must never return to this village.

117
00:13:58,220 --> 00:13:59,460
 You're banished!

118
00:13:59,460 --> 00:14:03,420
 That's...

119
00:14:03,420 --> 00:14:11,920
 But big fish were not the only food for the Vikings.

120
00:14:11,920 --> 00:14:14,910
 One morning, Maestro and his young friends...

121
00:14:14,910 --> 00:14:20,160
 Do you realize how high those salmon are jumping?

122
00:14:20,160 --> 00:14:29,200
 I've got an idea. Try these.

123
00:14:29,200 --> 00:14:43,190
 Now look, why don't we do it like the fish? Like this.

124
00:14:43,190 --> 00:14:47,230
 Or maybe like that, or even...

125
00:14:47,230 --> 00:14:49,890
 Why sure, why not?

126
00:14:49,890 --> 00:14:52,530
 Why didn't I think of it sooner?

127
00:14:52,530 --> 00:14:54,650
 Eureka! I found it! I found it!

128
00:14:54,650 --> 00:14:56,650
 I found it! Oh wow!

129
00:14:56,650 --> 00:14:58,590
 I found it by Cracky, I found it!

130
00:14:58,590 --> 00:15:01,470
 Just copy the salmon!

131
00:15:01,470 --> 00:15:03,470
 We can jump right over whatever's in the way!

132
00:15:03,470 --> 00:15:06,470
 Why don't we do what Maestro says? Let's try!

133
00:15:06,470 --> 00:15:09,770
 Hey!

134
00:15:09,770 --> 00:15:16,550
 There's something in the way! Let's disembark here!

135
00:15:16,550 --> 00:15:28,600
 The boat on these logs! And roll it!

136
00:15:28,600 --> 00:15:32,190
 Take it! Keep going! Keep going!

137
00:15:32,190 --> 00:16:11,940
 Their method was light boats. No reason we couldn't attack the upland villages, eh?

138
00:16:12,480 --> 00:16:13,220
 Is there?

139
00:16:28,290 --> 00:16:43,980
 Just what do we do?

140
00:16:44,030 --> 00:16:44,670
 Lucky thing.

141
00:16:45,740 --> 00:16:47,660
 We'll show them whose town this is.

142
00:17:31,720 --> 00:17:55,620
 Dead chief's side replaced his weapons, shields, his jewels, and the food he'll need during his long voyage.

143
00:17:55,900 --> 00:17:58,250
 His eldest son has the honor of lighting the pyre.

144
00:17:58,250 --> 00:18:04,510
 No, there's nothing sad here, for Odin, god of warriors, has a place of honor waiting for he who dies a brave death.

145
00:18:05,020 --> 00:18:08,740
 And revenge was taken over and over in a vicious cycle of plunder and attack.

146
00:18:09,180 --> 00:18:10,940
 Land became one vast battlefield.

147
00:18:20,160 --> 00:18:21,880
 Looting is forbidden among the Norsemen.

148
00:18:22,080 --> 00:18:24,160
 Instead, let's work together to build great ships

149
00:18:24,160 --> 00:18:26,380
 so that we can travel far to find food,

150
00:18:26,520 --> 00:18:28,080
 women, wealth, slaves, and land

151
00:18:28,080 --> 00:18:29,260
 for our children to kill.

152
00:18:39,280 --> 00:18:42,010
 One.

153
00:19:01,160 --> 00:19:14,300
 92.

154
00:19:14,460 --> 00:19:16,240
 Do hold your horses.

155
00:19:16,380 --> 00:19:17,000
 How true.

156
00:19:17,760 --> 00:19:22,590
 Steam planks on either side riveted together,

157
00:19:22,730 --> 00:19:23,930
 overlapping slightly.

158
00:19:24,850 --> 00:19:55,110
 Ships are ready.

159
00:19:55,290 --> 00:19:56,470
 Now you must elect a chief.

160
00:19:56,770 --> 00:19:59,390
 One of these candidates must lead the first expedition.

161
00:19:59,750 --> 00:20:00,570
 Make your choice.

162
00:20:05,680 --> 00:20:10,340
 Must all swear loyalty to him.

163
00:20:11,040 --> 00:20:11,610
 Go ahead.

164
00:20:13,230 --> 00:20:14,940
 Chief! Long live the chief!

165
00:20:16,150 --> 00:20:17,200
 Do it!

166
00:20:19,420 --> 00:20:22,490
 Everything all right?

167
00:20:22,490 --> 00:20:25,520
 It might work, but I'll be needing some cow hair!

168
00:20:25,520 --> 00:20:27,390
 There you go!

169
00:20:27,390 --> 00:20:29,370
 That's my stroke!

170
00:20:29,370 --> 00:20:36,040
 Everything seemed to check out fine. 23 meters 33 by 5 meters 25.

171
00:20:36,040 --> 00:20:39,740
 Height 1 meter 95, water level 85 centimeters.

172
00:20:39,740 --> 00:20:42,350
 Or locks 1 meter apart. Looks fine.

173
00:20:42,350 --> 00:20:44,970
 Length of oars? 4 meters 50.

174
00:20:44,970 --> 00:20:46,970
 My, look how solid those shields look.

175
00:20:46,970 --> 00:20:49,540
 Good, now let's get her into the water.

176
00:20:49,540 --> 00:20:51,450
 Fine. Load the horses!

177
00:20:51,450 --> 00:21:04,010
 Will do, most definitely. That will not do.

178
00:21:04,010 --> 00:21:06,900
 What if we tried it this way?

179
00:21:06,900 --> 00:21:08,500
 No, the poor thing would get dizzy.

180
00:21:08,500 --> 00:21:09,420
 How about that?

181
00:21:09,420 --> 00:21:09,920
 Hop!

182
00:21:09,920 --> 00:21:10,970
 A little risky.

183
00:21:10,970 --> 00:21:12,320
 What if he jumps in?

184
00:21:12,320 --> 00:21:14,760
 What if he falls overboard and...

185
00:21:17,020 --> 00:21:19,980
 That's it, that's the answer!

186
00:21:19,980 --> 00:21:23,750
 There, my friends, bring back the horse now.

187
00:21:23,750 --> 00:21:26,690
 Just tip the boat, and he'll climb aboard easily.

188
00:21:26,690 --> 00:21:29,150
 How did I ever get such an idea?

189
00:21:29,150 --> 00:21:39,330
 Begins. Each ship contains 2,500 kilos of provisions, 400 kilos of weapons, and 40 men.

190
00:21:39,330 --> 00:21:41,880
 Each man's coffer will double as his seat while he rows.

191
00:21:41,880 --> 00:21:45,880
 And remember, no fires on board, eat your food cold, and here's my latest invention.

192
00:21:45,880 --> 00:21:46,920
 It will help you navigate.

193
00:21:47,330 --> 00:21:49,070
 With it, you'll keep right on course.

194
00:21:49,460 --> 00:21:50,570
 Fine, we're about ready.

195
00:21:50,770 --> 00:21:51,450
 Want to come with us?

196
00:21:51,650 --> 00:21:52,650
 You're a man now.

197
00:22:02,430 --> 00:22:11,620
 Frog as thick as this one, it's not easy to use Maestro's instrument.

198
00:22:11,960 --> 00:22:13,860
 The chief decides to release some crows.

199
00:22:14,820 --> 00:22:18,170
 Come back, we've got to be close to shore.

200
00:22:19,680 --> 00:22:22,090
 The year is 793.

201
00:22:22,330 --> 00:22:25,190
 The island of Lindisfarne off the coast of Northumbria in northern England

202
00:22:25,190 --> 00:22:27,580
 was the site of the first Viking ring.

203
00:22:47,780 --> 00:22:49,320
 Joyce in the grave.

204
00:22:49,320 --> 00:22:58,210
 Now it's up to you, my youngest, to go in search of new treasures.

205
00:22:58,780 --> 00:22:59,280
 Okay.

206
00:23:00,190 --> 00:23:03,660
 Farewell, son, and good luck.

207
00:23:04,390 --> 00:23:15,970
 830, the Vikings headed for Ireland.

208
00:23:16,880 --> 00:23:19,700
 Torgs Hill, the Norwegian chief, decided to make it his own.

209
00:23:20,120 --> 00:23:22,460
 In 839, he was proclaimed King of Ulster.

210
00:23:23,050 --> 00:23:25,490
 By 840, the Vikings ruled the Low Countries,

211
00:23:26,200 --> 00:23:29,310
 and the Danes had sailed up the Seine River as far as Rouen.

212
00:23:32,650 --> 00:23:35,390
 843, it's Midsummer's Day in the French city of Nantes.

213
00:23:44,170 --> 00:23:50,340
 44, the Vikings followed the River Garonne into Toulouse on their way to Spain.

214
00:23:50,820 --> 00:23:54,300
 In 845, 120 ships sailed up the Seine and sacked Paris.

215
00:23:54,910 --> 00:23:58,000
 In 850, it was the Thames, the Sennegan, and the Loire.

216
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,440
 856 and 57, Paris was in flames.

217
00:24:00,950 --> 00:24:04,250
 In 859, the conqueror Hastings took his 62 ships and left the same.

218
00:24:04,590 --> 00:24:08,350
 His ships rounded the coast of Spain and sailed past Gibraltar to conquer the Moors in Morocco.

219
00:24:08,730 --> 00:24:11,810
 They sacked Murcia, the Balearic Islands of Russo and Camargue in France.

220
00:24:12,030 --> 00:24:14,110
 The next goal was Italy, Pisa and Luna.

221
00:24:14,780 --> 00:24:17,940
 But here at last, they met with solid resistance.

222
00:24:20,250 --> 00:24:25,190
 Got an idea. If you disguise yourself behind your weapons...

223
00:24:25,190 --> 00:24:33,630
 Beloved chief, the noble Hastings was killed in battle.

224
00:24:34,230 --> 00:24:36,790
 His last wish was to have a pious burial.

225
00:24:36,950 --> 00:24:38,710
 All his treasures will go to the church.

226
00:24:39,280 --> 00:24:40,160
 I'm sent.

227
00:25:07,590 --> 00:25:10,730
 Roman turn would be burned.

228
00:25:15,720 --> 00:25:19,320
 In 862, Hastings made his triumphal return to his base in Ratmoutier,

229
00:25:19,650 --> 00:25:21,700
 where his war plunder was shown to all.

230
00:25:22,840 --> 00:25:26,520
 In the same year, the Vikings left Sweden to fly Russia's rivers.

231
00:25:27,030 --> 00:25:29,170
 The Volkov, the Dnieper, the Volga.

232
00:25:29,920 --> 00:25:32,320
 Rurik founded Kiev, the first Russian state.

233
00:25:32,320 --> 00:25:35,820
 At about that time, the Varangians had reached Constantinople.

234
00:25:36,610 --> 00:25:39,410
 870. East England was conquered by the Danes.

235
00:25:40,610 --> 00:25:46,580
 874. Fair-haired Harold succeeded in unifying the Norwegians, who had soon found Reykjavik in Iceland.

236
00:25:48,180 --> 00:25:51,230
 880. France, Belgium, and Germany were invaded.

237
00:25:52,020 --> 00:25:54,260
 Viking ships wreaked havoc all along the Seine.

238
00:25:54,500 --> 00:25:56,840
 The Somme, the Schelp, the Meuse, the Rhine.

239
00:25:58,440 --> 00:26:00,480
 885. Paris was under siege.

240
00:26:00,840 --> 00:26:03,580
 700 ships were deployed for a distance of over 3 kilometers.

241
00:26:13,350 --> 00:26:17,290
 Charles the Fat bought the retreat of the invaders for 700 pounds of silver.

242
00:26:17,290 --> 00:26:20,860
 In 9-11, Charles the Simple gave the Norman chieftain Rolo a present.

243
00:26:21,120 --> 00:26:23,450
 Normandy, a region already familiar with Vikings.

244
00:26:24,320 --> 00:26:25,940
 Rolo pledged allegiance to the king.

245
00:26:29,130 --> 00:26:31,450
 982, Eric the Red sets foot in Greenland.

246
00:26:31,910 --> 00:26:34,900
 Other explorers get as far as Newfoundland and the American continent.

247
00:26:35,980 --> 00:26:38,780
 1028, Newt the Great reigned over Denmark, Norway, and England.

248
00:26:39,340 --> 00:26:42,020
 1059, Robert Grusgard became Duke of Puglia in Calabria.

249
00:26:42,440 --> 00:26:44,920
 1061, with his father, he snatched Sicily away from the Arabs.

250
00:26:45,200 --> 00:26:48,240
 This time, it was the Normans who arrived in Constantinople by way of the Balkans.

251
00:26:48,240 --> 00:26:51,180
 William of Normandy landed in England to claim the throne.

252
00:26:51,220 --> 00:27:00,700
 King Harald came to meet him. The result was the Battle of Hastings, and William became King of England.

253
00:27:03,470 --> 00:27:06,450
 The Vikings were warriors, but also traders and colonizers.

254
00:27:07,130 --> 00:27:09,950
 They were instrumental in extending the limits of the world of their time,

255
00:27:10,520 --> 00:27:13,640
 and so their voyage through history was more than just a series of bloody battles.

